  "doc_revision": "Eastron SDM630Modbus Smart Meter Modbus Protocol Implementation V1.8 (22-page PDF, copy hosted by the Victron community archive). The document does NOT say 'V2'.",
  "verified": false,
  "notes": "IMPORTANT: the only register document I could actually read is the SDM630Modbus protocol V1.8 (above); it does not mention the 'V2' hardware, and I could not find or read a protocol document explicitly for SDM630-Modbus V2. The V2 user manual (docs.vekto.nl) was not used for registers. Treat this preset as 'SDM630Modbus protocol V1.8' and check against your V2 unit. Addressing: the doc lists register numbers (3xxxx input / 4xxxx holding) plus the protocol start address in hex; this preset uses address_base 0 and the PROTOCOL address = input register number minus 30001 (30073 -> 72 = 0x0048) / holding minus 40001; every address was cross-checked against the doc's hex column (read from the rendered tables because the PDF text extraction is scrambled). Data: 32-bit IEEE754 float, 'most significant register first (Default)' unless the 'Register Order' holding parameter is changed (that parameter's address is not listed in the doc, so it is not included). Doc limits: max 40 values (80 registers) per request, even-aligned pairs only. NOTE the tool's block merger allows up to 125 registers per read, which exceeds this device limit; read by group or point. The doc's table has per-wiring-system columns (3P4W/3P3W/1P2W): parameters crossed out for a wiring system return 0. Defaults per the doc: node 1, no parity/1 stop bit (code 0), baud code 2 = 9600 (default); selectable 2400/4800/9600/19200/38400. Left out: password entry register 40025 (0x0018) , 'Reset' register 461457 (0xF010, write-only), Register Order (no address). 'System type' is marked r/w but the doc says it requires the password first. In the doc's units column, row 'Maximum total system power demand' says VA and 'Total system power demand' says W; taken as printed. Not verified against a physical device. max_block=40: the protocol document limits one request to 40 values (80 registers).",
